You need to employ better separation of concerns / SRP here.
At a high level, you could break this hook into:

1.) A generic `useIncident` hook that accepts only an incident id (and accepts them as refs for reactivity).

This hook would handle the state for the singular given incident. This would include the logic of ensuring the given incident gets loaded into the store (preferably in a lazy manor or configurably lazy manor).
Its return could look something like:
```ts
interface UseIncident {
  incident: ComputedRef<Incident | undefined>
  incidentId: ReadonlyRef<number> // the input incident id as ro
  hasIncident: ComputedRef<boolean> // Boolean(incident)
  isLoading: ComputedRef<boolean> // is incident currently being fetched
}
```

2.) A `useUserIncident(user: MaybeRef<User | undefined>, incidentId?: MaybeRef<number>)` hook, which handles:
 1.) exposing the given users incident id from state as computed
 2.) reacts to changes on the input `incident` ref to update user state accordingly.

and could return something like:
```ts
interface UseUserIncident {
  userIncidentId: ComputedRef<number | undefined>;
  hasUserIncidentId: ComputedRef<boolean> // do we have an incident from the user?
}
```


3.) A `useCurrentIncident` hook that tracks the current incident.
At a high level, this would make sure the other hooks to look something like:
```ts
const useCurrentIncident = (user?: MaybeRef<User|undefined>) => {
  user ??= useCurrentUser().currentUser;
  const incidentIdRouteParam = useRouteParams(...);
  const hasIncidentIdRoute = computedEager(() => Boolean(incidentIdRouteParam.value));
  const incidentIdFromRoute = computed(() => hasIncidentIdRoute.value ? Number(incidentIdRouteParam) : undefined//etc);

  const currentIncidentId = ref<number | undefined>();

  const { userIncidentId, hasUserIncidentId } = useUserIncident(user, currentIncidentId);

  const { incident, isLoading } = useIncident(currentIncidentId);

  const hasNewIncidentRoute = computed(() => hasIncidentIdRoute.value && incidentIdFromRoute.value !== userIncidentId.value);
  
  // populate or update from route
  whenever(hasNewIncidentRoute, () => {
   if(newValue !== currentIncidentId.value) {
			currentIncidentId.value = incidentIdRoute.value;
		}
  }, {immediate: true});

  // populate from user
  whenever(() => hasUserIncidentId.value && !hasIncidentIdRoute.value, () => {
    if(newValue !== currentIncidentId.value) { currentIncidentId.value = userIncidentId.value }
	});

  // fallback
  whenever(() => user.value && !hasIncidentIdRoute.value && !hasUserIncidentId.value && !currentIncidentId.value && !isLoading.value, async () => {
     const recentIncidentId = <fetch recent incident id>;
     currentIncidentId.value = recentIncidentId;
  });

  return { incident, isLoading }
};
```

I got a bit more detailed then intended that last part, but hopefully it gives you a better idea of what Im aiming for here.

